WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.612 --> 00:00:07.070
HARISH JAYAKUMAR:
Hello, everybody.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:08.695
Welcome to our session,
and today we're

00:00:08.695 --> 00:00:11.380
going to be talking about how
to enhance your security posture

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:15.320
and run PCI compliant
application with Anthos.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.239
I'm Harish Jayakumar.

00:00:16.239 --> 00:00:18.670
I lead the Application
Modernization team

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:19.900
here at Google Cloud.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:22.125
And today, with me, I have Ann.

00:00:22.125 --> 00:00:24.250
ANN WALLACE: Hi, I'm Ann
Wallace, Security Solution

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:26.815
Manager with Google Cloud.

00:00:26.815 --> 00:00:28.190
HARISH JAYAKUMAR:
Thank you, Ann.

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:29.690
Let's jump in.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:32.299
So when we talk about
running regulated workloads,

00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:34.127
it can get quite
complex, especially

00:00:34.127 --> 00:00:35.960
because there are
multiple stakeholders that

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:36.890
are involved in it.

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:38.270
On one side you have
the makers, that's

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:40.520
the developers, the product
owners, and the enterprise

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:41.060
architects.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:42.935
And on the other side,
you have the checkers,

00:00:42.935 --> 00:00:46.010
which are the security teams,
risk and compliance team,

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:47.880
and the legal teams as well.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.850
So because of
these complexities,

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.040
regulated workloads
are the slowest ones

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.990
to move to the Cloud,
or even to modernize.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:56.960
So in this talk, we're
going to be exploring

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.540
how to modernize these
workloads, at the same time,

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.630
meet the compliance
requirements that either need

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:04.459
to be on Google Cloud, or
if you want to run them

00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:07.400
on prem using Anthos.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.782
Now, running a regulated
workload, especially for PCI

00:01:10.782 --> 00:01:12.990
in this case, what we're
talking about with this one,

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:14.120
is challenging.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.570
Because audits can
be quite daunting.

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:17.660
Why?

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.760
One, compliance
frameworks were not

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.120
built for cloud
native architectures.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.870
Two, it actually takes a
village to pass an audit

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:27.890
and demonstrate the
compliance associated with it.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.820
Then three, you want to also
avoid misconfigurations.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:35.440
And if there are any, you have
to fix that And then finally,

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:37.412
evidencing a shared
responsibility, especially

00:01:37.412 --> 00:01:39.370
today when we talk with
customers, most of them

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:42.340
either have hybrid model or
they're on multiple clouds,

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:44.770
so understanding that
shared responsibility

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:47.248
becomes very, very important.

00:01:47.248 --> 00:01:49.040
Which brings you to
the slide where we are.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.575
How does this fit into the
shared responsibility model?

00:01:51.575 --> 00:01:53.450
So when we're looking
at simply, for example,

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:55.640
running Anthos on Google
Cloud, or if you're

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.970
running Anthos on prem, you can
see the difference over there.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:01.550
Simply running on Anthos
Cloud, you see a lot of it

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:04.130
is actually handed to
you by Google itself.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:05.690
And the customer
only needs to focus

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:08.060
on the application, some
configuration associated

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:10.550
with that, and some base images.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:12.950
Whereas the picture
completely changes when

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:14.300
you move to Anthos on prem.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:15.967
It is much more
controlled, the customer

00:02:15.967 --> 00:02:17.383
has to take care
of [? updating ?]

00:02:17.383 --> 00:02:19.350
the node hardware, the
networking, the storage,

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:21.060
and the physical hosting.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:24.560
So we need to be able to see,
for example, if something

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.920
happens, something goes wrong,
who's responsible for it.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.750
Let's take an example,
for example, when

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:31.205
an identity management is done.

00:02:31.205 --> 00:02:33.080
Whereas on the Google
Cloud, you can actually

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:34.970
leverage the Google Cloud, and
[? then repeat ?] some form

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:37.070
on the other with
Cloud IAM, and then we

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:39.420
can handle that for you.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.610
So customers are
starting to adopt

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:44.520
Anthos to build the next
generation digital platforms,

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.950
modernizing the security for
cloud native deployments is

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.320
a great use case where
they're actually using it.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.960
And the key objectives
for those modernizations,

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.330
are one, increasing the
developer productivity

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.940
through the use of containers
and micro services,

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.060
while adopting
hybrid and multicloud

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:00.900
as deployment platforms.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.040
Many of these customers
process sensitive data

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.710
that include PII, PCI, and PHI.

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:10.030
Further, they must also meet
the regulatory compliance.

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:13.200
But as application teams
adopt new technology stacks

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.110
and development processes,
using the existing paradigms

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:19.240
and processes is no longer
optimal or even sufficient.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.690
So in order to deliver
the developer agility,

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:24.185
the security stack
also needs to evolve

00:03:24.185 --> 00:03:26.310
to align with the new
development and [? product ?]

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:27.450
processes.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.780
It must also address new threats
that apply to modern production

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.210
environments.

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:35.720
Now, let's pivot quickly to
the compliance requirements.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.240
So we've taken some of
the compliance frameworks,

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:42.920
such as NIST 800-53, which
maps to [INAUDIBLE],, PCI DSS,

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:46.610
ISO 27001, and have identified
some common technical

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:47.780
requirements.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:50.840
They actually fall into
these five categories--

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:53.300
segmentation and isolation,
identity access management,

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:56.150
data security, supply
chain, application security.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:58.520
Now, we don't have time
to dive into all these

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.440
in this 25 minutes
talk, but we will

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:02.990
talk about how Anthos
can help with several

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:05.180
of these requirements
right out of the box.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.270
As you can see, the
same five requirements,

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:10.190
we have put them down
into five pillars.

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:12.710
And out of the
box, Anthos offers

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:15.050
a lot of security capabilities.

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:17.750
Each control aligns with the
common compliance requirements

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:19.542
that we talked about
in the previous slide,

00:04:19.542 --> 00:04:22.430
whether it's going to be
segmentation networking, IAM

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:25.250
data security and encryption,
secure supply chain,

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:26.662
and application security.

00:04:26.662 --> 00:04:28.370
So as you can see,
most of these features

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:30.470
to help you achieve
your security posture.

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:33.260
But as I mentioned before,
it's the shared responsibility

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:36.600
model, and you need to know how
to blend the controls together

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.616
appropriately and understand
what you are responsible for.

00:04:39.616 --> 00:04:41.780
For that, what we we're
going to be introducing

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:43.250
is Anthos Blueprints.

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:45.350
And to talk in
detail about that,

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:47.050
I'd like to call back Ann.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:48.700
Ann?

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:50.935
ANN WALLACE: Cool,
thanks Harish.

00:04:50.935 --> 00:04:52.810
So before we dive into
the Anthos Blueprints,

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:55.540
first, let's talk about
our security blueprint.

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:57.940
So we've designed
security blueprint

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:01.410
to help some of this
complexity that Harish

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:03.490
described, to simplify it.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:05.920
So the first
security blueprint we

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:07.610
have is called a
security posture.

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:09.760
So this is a collection
of resources and code

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:12.790
that deliver granular
and specific security

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:15.460
postures to a workload, such
as maybe a customer facing

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:17.620
application or back end process.

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:22.120
For example, securing data,
or securing API endpoints.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:24.530
The next one that we offer
is called a landing zone.

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:26.710
So this is a
pre-configured environment

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:30.050
with a standard set of secure
cloud Infrastructure, policy,

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:30.943
and guidelines.

00:05:30.943 --> 00:05:33.360
So this can be something that's
otherwise very complicated

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:35.830
to set up and might take
months of investigation,

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:37.270
implementation by
trial and error

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:38.480
to get right the first time.

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:41.650
So an example, this
PCI for Anthos.

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:43.770
Regardless of the type
of security blueprint,

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:46.450
they're all designed to
be easy to use and deploy.

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:49.360
And we also employ all
of Google Cloud Security

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:51.040
best practices in
the blueprints.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:54.890
So you can say they're
secure by default.

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:59.140
So one of the first security
landing zones that we released

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:04.390
was PCI on GKE, and coming
soon as PIC on Anthos.

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:06.790
So these help customers
get started with compliance

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:09.110
on Anthos quicker.

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:11.470
So it also allows you to
quickly and easily deploy

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:16.090
workloads on Anthos that align
to PCI DSS in a repeatable,

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:17.420
supported, and secure way.

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:18.837
So let's look at
everything that's

00:06:18.837 --> 00:06:21.400
included in the blueprint.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:24.220
So the PCI on Anthos
Blueprint contains

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:27.130
many opinionated reference
architecture diagrams

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:29.710
depicting the infrastructure
and application deployment.

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:32.350
While they're intended to be
used with deployable assets,

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.450
they also show you
how to create diagrams

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:37.360
of your containerized
cardholder data environments,

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:40.840
and the boundaries between PCI
and non-PCI workloads, which

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:43.300
is a requirement in PCI audits.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:46.410
The Blueprint also contains
different deployable assets.

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:50.800
And then we also include
PCI DSS 3.2.1 mappings

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:53.560
to the solutions, and a
compliance white paper.

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:59.430
So with our Anthos
on GCP Blueprint,

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:01.470
we provide you with
Terraform templates

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:03.270
to set up your infrastructure.

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:06.400
These include org
policies, projects, VPCs,

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:07.860
and GKE clusters.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:11.280
And as Harish described
about the shift in the shared

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:14.190
responsibility model,
on prem we only

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:15.960
provide you with
reference architectures

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:18.372
for squares one and two.

00:07:18.372 --> 00:07:19.830
But with both of
the blueprints, we

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:24.240
provide you with service mesh
profiles, GKE configurations,

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:26.760
ACM policies, and
then a demo app

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:29.640
to test out the environment
configuration and setup.

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:34.840
So let's pivot to segmentation.

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:36.800
There are two types
of segmentation.

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:40.000
So the first one is
macro segmentation.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.920
So these are things that
you do outside the cluster.

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:44.860
So you segment the
users, networks,

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:48.220
and applications using
different Google Cloud features

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:49.060
and tools.

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:52.210
This really helps you set
aside the scope of the audit.

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:54.010
And then we have
micro segmentation.

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:56.450
So these are things that
you do inside the cluster.

00:07:56.450 --> 00:08:00.380
So let's double click
on that a little bit.

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:04.720
So with GKE micro segmentation,
we offer many ways to do this.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:06.340
So we have network policies.

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:10.130
This helps with layer 3 and
4 network level segmentation.

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:14.320
Then we have GKE namespaces
for virtual clusters

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:17.970
to segment teams, or
maybe app versus prod,

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:19.420
or app dev versus prod.

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:21.340
Then we have GKE
private clusters,

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:23.530
so you have clusters
of private IP space.

00:08:23.530 --> 00:08:26.010
And then Anthos service mesh.

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:28.150
Then the net net here
is there are many ways

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:32.326
to do micro segmentation
in a cluster.

00:08:32.326 --> 00:08:36.090
So for the PCI on Anthos
on prem blueprint,

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:38.100
the first layer of
segmentation is addressed

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:40.520
through separate clusters.

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:43.610
So we create two clusters,
two user clusters,

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:47.180
one for in-scope, or for
your cardholder data cluster,

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:51.500
and then one for the
out-of-scope workloads.

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:54.530
And then building
upon the segmentation,

00:08:54.530 --> 00:08:57.240
we have segmented out of
the pods within a cluster,

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:00.020
but you further need to
demonstrate separation

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:02.390
of maybe dev and test
versus prod environments

00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:06.030
to your auditor, or you need to
segment application profiles.

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:10.797
So this is where you would
use different namespaces.

00:09:10.797 --> 00:09:13.130
And then oftentimes, you're
presented with a requirement

00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:15.800
to protect the routing
information and the private IP

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:16.790
spaces.

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:19.630
This is where you mark
a cluster as private.

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:21.590
And such a cluster
and the master

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:24.992
become inaccessible from
the public internet.

00:09:24.992 --> 00:09:26.700
And then the nodes in
the private cluster

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:30.040
do not have public IP addresses.

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:31.890
So the admin and
user clusters are

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:34.860
private for the on prem
blueprint and the GCP

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:37.240
blueprint.

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:39.490
So looking at some of the
reference architectures that

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:42.520
are provided as part
of this blueprint,

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:45.790
you can see here that again,
the in-scope and out-of-scope

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:47.920
cluster separation.

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:49.480
And then we have
Anthos Service Mesh,

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:52.510
so it's a single mesh
for multi clusters.

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:54.760
And then our demo app is
called Online Boutique.

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:59.058
So this is an e-commerce micro
services demo application.

00:09:59.058 --> 00:10:00.850
So this allows you to
test out the controls

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:04.750
and the architecture.

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:07.670
The PCI DSS audit
burden is minimized

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:11.990
by moving the micro services
from that demo application that

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:15.950
don't touch payment card data
into the out-of-scope cluster.

00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:18.650
This reference architecture
details this layout.

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:20.780
This also helps you with
your own architectures

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:23.858
and documentation.

00:10:23.858 --> 00:10:25.400
And then looking
more specifically at

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:26.630
PCI requirements.

00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:30.050
So PCI requirement four
talks about encrypting

00:10:30.050 --> 00:10:32.510
data in flight, specifically
of cardholder data

00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:35.180
across open and public networks.

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:37.940
For our on prem blueprint,
the in-scope and out-of-scope

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:39.590
clusters are in
separate networks,

00:10:39.590 --> 00:10:43.250
which helps with strong
isolation for PCI boundaries.

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:46.220
This also means that the cross
cluster communication must

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:48.680
go through a Istio gateway.

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:52.880
All cross customer communication
uses mutual TLS authentication

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:54.200
and encryption.

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:56.150
Citadel is also
included for managing

00:10:56.150 --> 00:10:59.900
the internal certificates.

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:02.120
So a lot of the blueprint
is for day zero,

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:04.580
but we also want to talk
about day one and day

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:06.090
two sort of scenarios.

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:09.200
So this is where policy as
code or shifting left happen.

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:12.840
So setting up initially
is great, everything's PCI

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:13.340
compliant.

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:15.658
But as you're deploying
new containers or new code

00:11:15.658 --> 00:11:17.450
into your environment,
how do you make sure

00:11:17.450 --> 00:11:20.460
these meet PCI requirements?

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:23.900
So to help with this, we
have binary authorization,

00:11:23.900 --> 00:11:26.930
Anthos Config Management,
and Anthos Policy Controller.

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:31.240
So let's look at these in
a little bit more detail.

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:33.810
So for a requirements such
as establish a process

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:36.700
to identify security
vulnerabilities,

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:40.050
you can use Container Registry
Vulnerability Scanning.

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:42.030
So this checks the
images that you

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:45.450
push into the registry for
known security vulnerabilities.

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:48.390
It is constantly refreshing
its vulnerability database

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:51.290
by ingesting corresponding
vendor security feeds,

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:54.240
so that you can be notified
of new vulnerabilities that

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:57.870
maybe previously
affected scan containers.

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:01.170
You can also tie this in with
Pub/Sub to get notifications,

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:04.080
so you can integrate this
in with your CICD process,

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:09.610
or file bugs for newly
discovered vulnerabilities.

00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:12.760
And another requirement
is to ensure all systems

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:15.140
and software are protected
from known vulnerabilities.

00:12:15.140 --> 00:12:17.300
This is where binary
authorization can help.

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:19.330
So it ensures that
only properly signed

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:21.130
containers are deployed
into production

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:23.620
or other environments.

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:26.590
You can use binary authorization
to create deployment policies

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:29.060
based on container
image metadata.

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:31.540
So these policies can
be based on attestation,

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:34.200
such as a vulnerability scan,
or other types of signatures,

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:36.850
such as maybe being built
by a particular pipeline,

00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:40.030
or having gone through
a specific QA process.

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:42.850
Again, this ensures that only
trusted images are deployed

00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:45.858
into your environments.

00:12:45.858 --> 00:12:47.150
So let's put all this together.

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:50.300
So when code is checked into
GitHub or your source code

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:53.420
repository, it triggers
a a build in Cloud Build.

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:56.280
Cloud Build then generates
a new container image.

00:12:56.280 --> 00:12:59.810
It will create a build record
that contains information

00:12:59.810 --> 00:13:02.990
on the image, including the
location, the source code

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:04.960
from which the image was built.

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:06.960
And then that triggers
a vulnerability scan.

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:09.170
So the vulnerability
scan is done, the results

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:11.300
of that scan are stored
as part of the metadata

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:12.700
for that container.

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:14.610
And as mentioned on
the previous slide,

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:16.340
you can configure
binary authorization

00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:20.550
to require attestation based on
the vulnerability scan results.

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:22.640
So this prevents images
with known vulnerabilities

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:25.970
beyond a certain level, so maybe
say high, from being deployed.

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:27.920
You can also have
other requirements,

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:30.800
like only allowing images
from trusted sources.

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:32.330
So if someone
tries to deploy one

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:35.030
of these unauthorized
images, binary authorization

00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:40.130
will stop it, and all of this
is logged in Cloud Audit Log.

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:43.360
So now Anthos Config
Management, or ACM.

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:46.070
So with ACM, you can create
common configurations

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:49.250
across all your infrastructure,
including custom policies,

00:13:49.250 --> 00:13:52.480
and apply it to both on
prem and across clouds.

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:54.637
Anthos Config Management
evaluates the changes

00:13:54.637 --> 00:13:56.720
and rolls them out to all
the Kubernetes clusters,

00:13:56.720 --> 00:14:00.780
so that your desired
state is always reflected.

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:03.730
And so based on Open
Policy Agent's Gateway,

00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:06.880
ACM Policy Controller provides
first class integration

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:10.640
between OPA and Kubernetes
via a custom controller.

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:13.668
So this turns Rego policies
into Kubernetes objects,

00:14:13.668 --> 00:14:15.460
allowing them to be
customized and deployed

00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:18.106
using your standard workflows.

00:14:18.106 --> 00:14:20.390
When policy controller
is configured,

00:14:20.390 --> 00:14:23.870
incoming objects can be denied
admission into your cluster,

00:14:23.870 --> 00:14:29.060
or audited to see if they
violate governance policies.

00:14:29.060 --> 00:14:31.900
So we offer quite a
few sample policies

00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:33.250
that you can use with ACM.

00:14:33.250 --> 00:14:35.320
They include policies
like required services

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:40.030
to disable unauthorized
access, or label enforcement.

00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:43.030
But more specifically, with
our Anthos PCI Blueprint,

00:14:43.030 --> 00:14:45.310
we include policies
to help enforce TLS.

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:48.520
And again, this helps
enforce PCI requirement four,

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:51.490
which talks about
encrypting data in transit,

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:54.520
specifically of cardholder
data across open and public

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:56.910
networks.

00:14:56.910 --> 00:14:58.530
So as you can see
from this slide,

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:00.940
looking at some other
common controls,

00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:03.600
we can see how services
like binary authorization

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:08.390
or Policy Controller and Anthos
Config Management can help.

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:10.970
And then lastly, we tie
all of this together

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:12.710
by mapping the
various components

00:15:12.710 --> 00:15:17.350
and policies in the
blueprint to PCI DSS 3.2.1.

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:19.950
And we also include
a PCI white paper.

00:15:19.950 --> 00:15:22.550
So the white paper provides
an independent third party

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:26.210
assessment of the blueprint
performed by PCI auditors.

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:28.670
This not only gives you, our
customers, more confidence

00:15:28.670 --> 00:15:29.820
in our solution.

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:32.120
It is also something that
you can provide your QSA

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:35.510
as part of your audit, which
hopefully will help speed up

00:15:35.510 --> 00:15:38.590
your assessment.

00:15:38.590 --> 00:15:42.610
So to learn more about this, or
our other Blueprint, schedule

00:15:42.610 --> 00:15:44.590
a session with your Google team.

00:15:44.590 --> 00:15:45.850
Or check out the links here.

00:15:45.850 --> 00:15:48.250
So there's one for
Google Cloud and PCI,

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:52.850
and one for our
security blueprints.

00:15:52.850 --> 00:15:54.610
Thank you.

